%S A400778 1,1,1,1,4,1,1,16,16,1,1,64,240,64,1,1,256,3584,3584,256,1,1,1024,
%T A400778 53504,196608,53504,1024,1,1,4096,798720,10747904,10747904,798720,
%U A400778 4096,1,1,16384,11923456,587202560,2138112000,587202560,11923456,16384,1,1,65536
%N A400778 Array read by antidiagonals: T(n,k) is the number of ways to place mirrors between neighboring dots of an n X k grid of dots so that the mirror curve is a single closed loop.
%C A400778 Put each dot in the center of a unit square, so the dots fill an n X k rectangle of squares. Each side shared by two squares either has a two-sided mirror on it or has no mirror. There are n*(k-1) + k*(n-1) such sides, so there are 2^(2*n*k-n-k) ways to place mirrors.
%C A400778 A ray of light starts at the midpoint of a side and moves at 45 degrees. It reflects off the border and off every mirror, and passes straight through every side without a mirror, until it returns to its start. Repeating this from every unused midpoint gives the mirror curve (Gerdes), a set of closed paths that together go around every dot.
%C A400778 T(n,k) counts the mirror placements for which the mirror curve is a single closed path.
%C A400778 Mirror curves of this kind describe the kolam designs of South India and the rangoli designs drawn at the festival of Diwali, as well as the sona sand drawings of the Chokwe people of Angola. A design that is one closed path can be drawn without lifting the hand.
%C A400778 With no mirrors, the mirror curve has gcd(n,k) closed paths (Jablan et al.).
%C A400778 In the 3 X 3 case, 48 of the 240 designs use 2 mirrors and the other 192 use 4 mirrors.
%H A400778 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A400778/b400778.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..136</a> (antidiagonals 1 to 16).
%H A400778 Paulus Gerdes, <a href="https://www.mi.sanu.ac.rs/vismath/paulus/pg1.htm">Mirror designs and mirror curves</a>, Visual Mathematics.
%H A400778 Slavik Jablan, Ljiljana Radovic, Radmila Sazdanovic and Ana Zekovic, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.3784">Mirror-curves and knot mosaics</a>, arXiv:1106.3784 [math.GT], 2011.
%H A400778 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A400778/a400778.png">Illustration of T(2,2) = 4 and of 10 of the T(3,3) = 240 designs</a>
%H A400778 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A400778/a400778_1.png">Single-loop designs on 9 X 9 and 11 X 11 grids</a>
